        "problem": "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$",
        "worked_solution": "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\u2032} & {y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{\u2032}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({e}^{x}){(x{e}^{x})}^{\u2032}-(x{e}^{x}){({e}^{x})}^{\u2032} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-(x{e}^{x})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}-x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
